1
00:00:01,572 --> 00:00:08,944
Welcome to Asia episode one of seven. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,610 --> 00:00:23,774
We begin at Asia’s western crossroads: Türkiye, Cyprus, Georgia, Armenia and
Azerbaijan. Cyprus counts once, with the island’s division explained at its
stop.

3
00:00:24,498 --> 00:00:27,609
Let's bring Turkey and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:40,339 --> 00:00:52,832
Türkiye, labelled Turkey in GeoClick, stretches across Anatolia between the
Black Sea and the Mediterranean. A smaller part lies in southeastern Europe,
beyond the Bosphorus.

5
00:00:53,492 --> 00:01:08,678
Istanbul grew beside that narrow water crossing. Its historic monuments
include the sixth-century Hagia Sophia: a reminder that this meeting point of
seas also connected empires and cultures.

6
00:01:09,335 --> 00:01:22,988
Remember the broad bridge between Europe and Asia: Black Sea above,
Mediterranean below, and Istanbul at the western crossing. Most of the country
lies on the Asian side.

7
00:01:23,763 --> 00:01:26,956
Let's bring Cyprus and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:37,985 --> 00:01:48,945
Cyprus is the island south of Türkiye and west of the Syrian and Lebanese
coast. Keep it offshore in the eastern Mediterranean, rather than attached to
the mainland.

9
00:01:49,601 --> 00:02:03,591
Paphos preserves ancient mosaics and a sanctuary associated with Aphrodite.
Her birth from the sea is a Greek myth linked to this island, not a historical
event.

10
00:02:05,136 --> 00:02:23,640
The island is divided by a United Nations buffer zone. GeoClick labels
Northern Cyprus separately; that northern entity is recognised only by
Türkiye. We count Cyprus once. Remember one island, south of Türkiye.

11
00:02:24,436 --> 00:02:27,977
Let's bring Georgia and its neighbours into view.

12
00:02:39,420 --> 00:02:49,660
Georgia lies at the eastern end of the Black Sea, below Russia and above
Armenia and Türkiye. Azerbaijan is its neighbour toward the Caspian side.

13
00:02:50,318 --> 00:03:04,563
Traditional Georgian wine-making uses large clay vessels called qvevri, buried
in the ground. Families pass down the skills for fermenting and storing wine
inside these egg-shaped pots.

14
00:03:05,217 --> 00:03:17,710
Picture a clay wine vessel beside the Black Sea end of the Caucasus. Georgia
has a Black Sea coast; Armenia, immediately south, does not.

15
00:03:20,575 --> 00:03:30,467
Armenia is landlocked, south of Georgia and east of Türkiye. Azerbaijan lies
to its east, while a short southern border reaches Iran.

16
00:03:31,149 --> 00:03:41,087
At Geghard, several churches and tombs were cut into the surrounding rock. The
monastery makes architecture and mountain landscape seem to grow together.

17
00:03:41,974 --> 00:03:53,062
Remember the inland country below Georgia: rock-cut churches in the mountains.
Do not give Armenia a Black Sea or Caspian coast; neither sea reaches it.

18
00:03:56,324 --> 00:04:11,893
Azerbaijan occupies the Caspian side of the southern Caucasus, east of Armenia
and southeast of Georgia. Its Nakhchivan exclave is separated from the main
territory by Armenia.

19
00:04:12,545 --> 00:04:24,225
Baku preserves an old walled city inside a much larger modern capital. Its
Maiden Tower and palace reflect the layers of history beside the Caspian
shore.

20
00:04:24,882 --> 00:04:36,736
Keep the pair apart: Georgia on the Black Sea, Azerbaijan on the Caspian,
Armenia inland between and below them. Think of Baku’s old walls at the
eastern end.

21
00:04:44,558 --> 00:04:59,930
Türkiye bridges the seas, Cyprus sits offshore to its south, and the three
Caucasus countries connect the Black Sea with the Caspian. Georgia is on the
Black Sea, Armenia inland, Azerbaijan on the Caspian.

22
00:05:00,586 --> 00:05:15,725
Recall Istanbul’s crossing, the island’s ancient mosaics, Georgia’s clay wine
vessels, Armenia’s rock-cut monastery and Baku’s old walls. These five stops
begin our seven-part route through Asia.
